"Deborah Rose Resume/CV | The University of New South Wales, Environmental Humanities Program, Adjunct (https://unsw.academia.edu/DeborahRose/CurriculumVitae)\nciteturn26view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://unsw.academia.edu/DeborahRose/CurriculumVitae\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 271\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Skip to main content L3: \nL4: [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Log In†www.academia.edu cite3†Sign Up†www.academia.edu L7:   * cite2†Log In†www.academia.edu L8:   * cite3†Sign Up†www.academia.edu L9:   * more\nL10:   *     * cite4†About†www.academia.edu L11:     * cite5†Press†www.academia.edu L12:     * cite6†Papers†www.academia.edu L13:     * cite7†Terms†www.academia.edu L14:     * cite8†Privacy†www.academia.edu L15:     * cite9†Copyright†www.academia.edu L16:     * cite10†We're Hiring!†www.academia.edu L17:     * cite11†Help Center†support.academia.edu L18:     * less\nL19: # Deborah Rose - Curriculum Vitae\nL20: \nL21: cite12†Deborah Rose L22: \nL23: The University of New South Wales, Environmental Humanities Program, Adjunct\nL24: \nL25: cite13†Download†www.academia.edu L26: CURRICULUM VITAE Name Deborah Bird Rose Contact Information: School of Humanities Morven Brown Building University of New South Wales NSW 2052 Australia Ph: +61287643492 cite14†[email protected] https://research.unsw.edu.au/people/professor-deborah-bird- rose http://deborahbirdrose.com Current Positions: Adjunct Professor, Environmental Humanities Program University of New South Wales, Sydney, NSW Associate Academic, Centre for Compassionate Conservation, University of Technology, Sydney, NSW Degrees 1984 Ph.D.\nL27: Anthropology. Bryn Mawr College, Bryn Mawr, PA (USA). 1977 M.A. Anthropology. Bryn Mawr College 1973 B.A. Anthropology (honors and distinction). University of Delaware, Newark, DE.\nL28: Current Research: Extinction Studies; Environmental Humanities; Multispecies Ethnographies; Symbiosis, Bio-inclusion and an Ethics of Complex Mutualism; Indigenous Philosophical Ecologies Professional Memberships: Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia, Fellow Association for the Study of Literature, Environment and Culture – Australia New Zealand Australian Anthropological Society, Fellow Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Studies, Member Ecological Humanities, Foundation member (www.ecologicalhumanities.org) Extinction Studies Working Group, Founding member (www.extinctionstudies.org ) Kangaloon – Creative Ecologies, Founding member (www.kangaloongroup.org) Current Editorial Positions: Routledge: Environmental Humanities Series Sydney University Press: Animal Publics Series Editorial Board, Environment and Planning D: Society and Space (UK) (A rank) 2 Current Advisory and Service Positions Advisory Board, RMIT Art and Sustainability Research Cluster Advisory Board, The Asia Pacific Journal of Anthropology (Australia)(A rank) Mak Mak Research Group, Honorary ‘KuniKuni’, White Eagle Association, Batchelor, NT Plumwood Trust Association Previous Editorial Positions Founder and Co-editor with Thom van Dooren of Environmental Humanities, now published by Duke University Press and dedicated to publishing current interdisciplinary research on the environment.\nL29: Co-editor with Thom van Dooren: ‘Ecological Humanities’ in the Australian Humanities Review, 2008-2012 Co-editor with Libby Robin: ‘Ecological Humanities’ in the Australian Humanities Review, 2004-2008.\nL30: Editorial Board, Aboriginal History Editorial Board, Australian Humanities Review (A rank) Editorial Board, Ethics, Place & Environment: A Journal of Philosophy & Geography Editorial Board, The Asia Pacific Journal of Anthropology Academic Summary 2013 (continuing): Professor (Adjunct), Environmental Humanities Program, School of Humanities, University of New South Wales, Sydney. 2015 (continuing): Academic Associate, Centre for Compassionate Conservation, UTS, Sydney.\nL31: 2008-2013: Professor of Social Inclusion, Centre for Research on Social Inclusion, Macquarie University, Sydney 2000-2008 Senior Fellow, Centre for Resource and Environmental Studies, Fenner School of Environment and Society, ANU, Canberra. 1998-2000 Senior Fellow, Anthropology Department, Research School of Pacific and Asian Studies, ANU 1995-8 Senior Fellow, North Australia Research Unit, ANU, Darwin. 1994 (July-December) Acting Executive Director, NARU, ANU, Darwin.\nL32: 1993 Visiting Fellow, NARU, ANU, Darwin. 1990-1 Visiting Fellow, Department of Anthropology and Prehistory, ANU; Field work, NT. 1989 Visiting Fellow, History Department (Arts), ANU; writing Hidden Histories. 1988 Visiting Fellow, Humanities Research Centre, ANU; writing Dingo Makes Us Human. 3 1984-8 Visiting Fellow, Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Studies.\nL33: Grants 2011-2014 ARC Discovery Project Grant, with Dr Thom van Dooren: ‘Encounters with Extinction: A multi-sited, multi-species approach to life at the edge of catastrophe in the Asia-Pacific region’ (DP110102886) 2010 Academy of Social Science in Australia - International Science Linkages Bilateral Program, with Professor Lin, Yih-ren: ‘Extinction in multi-species communities: moon bear case study’ 2009 Macquarie University ‘Safety Net’ Grant: ‘Extinctions in multi-species communities: ways of living and dying in the Anthropocene’ 2008 ASSA Workshop Grant with Kathie Gibson and Ruth Fincher: ‘An Ethics for Living in the Anthropocene’ 2005-2009 ARC Grant, with Libby Robin: ARC DP0665034: ‘Producing biodiversity’.\nL34: 2003 Research Grant, National Parks and Wildlife Service, NSW, ‘Research into Kinship with the Natural World, Stage 2’ 2002 Research Grant, National Parks and Wildlife Service, NSW, ‘’Totemism and the Social Context of Plants and Animals in NSW’. 2002-2004 with Libby Robin, ARC DP0208361: Nature and Nation: Science, Environment and National Identity in Australia.\nL35: (Research now completed) 1996, 1997, 1999 Grants from the Cooperative Research Centre for the Sustainable Development of Tropical Savannas in support of the Northern Landscapes Symposia. 1996 Grant from the Biodiversity Unit, Department of the Environment, Sport and Territories and the North Australia Research Unit, The Australian National University, Canberra and Darwin, to host the symposium ‘Country in Flames’. 1991 April-December.\nL36: Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Studies. Project: 'Culture and Ecology, Victoria River Downs Area'. Visiting Fellow, Department of Anthropology and Prehistory, Australian National University. 1990 Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Studies. Project: 'Dreaming Ecology, Outback Mythology, and Environmental Degradation: Victoria River Downs (NT)'.\nL37: Visiting Fellow, Department of Anthropology and Prehistory, Australian National University. 1989 Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies, writing Hidden Histories. Also Visiting Fellow, History Department, Australian National University. 1987 February-April. Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies, Project: 'Culture and Ecology, Victoria River Downs Area'. 4 1987 May/June.\nL38: Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies, Project: 'The Cultural Significance of Rock Art in the Victoria River District'(conducted jointly with Darrell Lewis and funded by a grant from the Department of Aboriginal Affairs). 1986 March-August. Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies. Research Project: 'Ethnobotanical Research in the area of Victoria River Downs'. 1983-4 December/January. Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies.\nL39: Project: 'Religious Identity in the Victoria River District'. 1980-2 Yarralin, NT. Research Grantee, Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies. Project: 'Cultural Identity'. 1980-2 National Science Foundation Grant for research in Aboriginal Australia Academic Awards and Appointments 2011 Judge for the Stanner Award, administered by the Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Studies. 2008 Judge in the competition for the Watermark Literary Fellowship.\nL40: 2007 Visiting Associate, Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences, University of Technology, Sydney, 2007-2008. 2005 Visiting Scholar in Taiwan, with lectures at Providence University and the Tung-hai University, sponsored by the National Science Council of Taiwan, November. 2005 Visiting Fellow, Humanities Research Centre, working on Indigenous Water Philosophy.\nL41: 2004 Visiting Scholar in Taiwan, sponsored by the National Science Council of Taiwan, with lectures at the National Dong Hwa University, the National Taitung University, and the National Park Division of the Construction and Planning Agency, Ministry of Interior. (November) 2001 Visiting Fellow at the Hawke Institute as part of the Big Questions and Global Answers: Geopolitics, Culture and Spirit series, October 22-28. 2000 July –December, Visiting Fellow, Humanities Research Centre, ANU.\nL42: Project: ‘Native Title: Life or Death?’ 1998 February, Visitor, Department of Philosophy, Latrobe University, Melbourne. 1997 October-November. Distinguished Visitor, Departments of Anthropology, History and Women’s Studies, University of Adelaide. 1994-6 Foundation Member, National Biodiversity Council 1993 Visiting Fellow, North Australia Research Unit, The Australian National University. 1988 Visiting Fellow, Humanities Research Centre, Australian National University, writing Dingo Makes Us Human.\nL43: 5 Teaching - Doctoral Students Currently Supervised / Advised  Tim Pearson, Macquarie (Co-Supervisor), Topic: Flying Fox Communication (Dept of Biological Sciences). Doctoral Students whose studies are completed  Dr Patrick Sullivan, Anthropology, Australian National University, (Adviser), Topic: ‘All free man now : culture and post-colonialism in the Kimberley Division North- Western Australia’, 1989.\nL44:  Dr Gertrude Stotz, Anthropology, Deakin University, (Supervisor), Topic: ‘\"Kurdungurlu got to drive Toyota\" : differential colonizing process among the Warlpiri’, 1993.  Dr Catherine Robinson, Department of Environmental Studies, Monash University, 1999, (Associate Supervisor)  Dr Minoru Hokari, Department of History, ANU, (Adviser).\nL45: Topic; Cross- culturalizing history : journey to the Gurindji way of historical practice’, 2001  George Main, CRES, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Cultural and Environmental History of the Western Slopes of NWS’, CRES, ANU 2004  Dr Anthea Dee, CRES, ANU (Advisor), Topic: ‘Seasonal habitat use by feral pigs (Sus scrofa) in the Arafura Wetlands and their impact on contemporary Aboriginal bush food resources’, 2004.\nL46:  Dr Bernadette Hince, CRES, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Environmental History of the Subantarctic Islands’, 2005  Dr Leah Gibbs, CRES, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Rethinking Water: Variability and the Lake Eyre Basin, Central Australia’, 2006  Dr Thom Van Dooren, CRES, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Seeding Property: Nature, Human/Plant Relations and the Production of Wealth’ 2007  Dr Jessica Weir, CRES, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Murray River Country: An ecological dialogue with Traditional Owners’ 2007  Dr Matt Hall, CRES, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Plants and Personhood: An Interdisciplinary Inquiry into a Philosophical Botany’ 2008  Dr Natasha Fijn, Anthropology, Faculties, ANU (Advisor) Topic: ‘Living with Herds in Mongolia’ 2008  Dr Ingereth Macfarlane, History, ANU (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Entangled Places: Interactive Histories in the west Simpson Desert, Central Australia’ 2010  Dr Stuart Cooke, Social Inclusion, Macquarie (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Speaking the Earth’s Languages: a theory for a trans-Pacific indigenous poetics’ 2011  Dr Marcus Baynes-Rock, Macquarie (Supervisor) Topic: ‘Hyenas Like Us: Social Relations with an Urban Carnivore in Harar, Ethiopia’  Dr Katherine Wright, Macquarie University (Co-Supervisor), Topic: Cross-Kingdom Stories: More-than-human encounters in the New England Tablelands, 2013.\nL47:  Dr Hau-ren Hung, Macquarie University (Supervisor), Topic: Core values for Cultural Survival: A comparison of case studies in Taiwan and Australia Recent master classes, workshops and symposia organised for PhD Candidates and Postdoctoral Fellows include: 6 2010 Masterclass for PhD Candidates and Post-doctoral Fellows, with Dr Marc Bekoff, University of Colorado (3 December, Macquarie University) 2009 Masterclass for PhD Candidates and Post-doctoral Fellows, with Professor James Hatley, University of Salisbury (9 July, Macquarie University) 2007 Symposium in honour of Eric Rolls (ANU, 4 December) 2007 Symposium: Master class on place with Kate Rigby (ANU, 27 September) 2006 Symposium: Flows, Stoppages and Pulses (ANU, 5 December) 2006 Death and Extinction in Multi-Species Environments (ANU, 9 October) 2004 Desecration: A Symposium (ANU, 14 May) 2004 Symposium: Multicentric Materialisations in Environmental Thought (20 July) 2003 ‘Humanities Perspectives on Sustainability’, Roundtable discussion (13 May) Undergraduate Teaching 2010-11-12-13, Co-convener and lecturer: The Ecological Humanities (ENVG 262), Macquarie University 1992 Lecturer, first semester, Department of Anthropology and Prehistory, ANU: 'Traditional Aboriginal Culture'.\nL48: 1987 Tutor (part-time), Department of Anthropology and Prehistory, Australian National University: 'Introduction to Anthropology'. 1980 January-May. Lecturer, Mainline School Night, Radnor, PA. 'Man & Woman - Cultural Dimensions of Gender'. 1979 June-December. Adjunct lecturer, Trenton State College, Trenton, NJ. 'Introduction to Anthropology'; 'Cultural and Social Change'.\nL49: 1973-4 Coordinator, Women's Education Collective, Women's Resource Centre, Newark, Delaware, Teaching 'Women and Our Bodies' 1970-1972 Teaching and Research Assistant, Department of Anthropology, University of Delaware. Recent Guest Teaching 2015 Lecture, Oberlin College, October 3 (skype) 2007 Lecturer, Schumacher College, Totnes, Devon, UK, short course: Indigenous Ecological Knowledge, March 5-9.\nL50: 2005 Four Lectures on Postcolonial Philosophical Ecologies for Settler Societies, Providence University, Taiwan, November. 2005 Lecturer in the IRP Honours Workshop, CRES, 14-15 July.\nL51: 7 1999 Convener, Workshop on Methods and Ethics for research with human subjects, Department of Anthropology, RSPAS, ANU Consulting Summary Since 1981 I have worked on more than twenty land claims, native title cases, and Aboriginal land disputes, in some cases working with the claimants and in other cases working for the Aboriginal Land Commissioner. I have carried out sacred sites surveys throughout the Victoria River District.\nL52: In the course of this work I have become an experienced bush woman: I have travelled by truck, foot, boat, and helicopter, have driven cross-country through sand and mud, across boulders and through the long grass, and have slept out in a wide variety of places and conditions. I have also worked as a consultant on matters concerning sacred sites in NSW, and have investigated ‘totemic landscapes’ in NSW for the National Parks and Wildlife Service.\nL53: In addition I have conducted commissioned research and writing for the Australian Heritage Commission, among others. A list of consultancies is available on request. Publications Books: Forthcoming (2016): Extinction Studies: Stories of Time, Death and Generations, eds. Deborah Bird Rose, Thom van Dooren and Matthew Chrulew, Columbia University Press. 2015 Manifesto for Living in the Anthropocene, Katherine Gibson, Deborah Bird Rose & Ruth Fincher, eds, punctum books, Brooklyn NY.\nL54: 2011 Wild Dog Dreaming: Love and Extinction, University of Virginia Press, Charlottesville. (Published in the series ‘Under the Sign of Nature: Explorations in Ecocriticism’; available on Kindle and in paperback) 2011 Country of the Heart: An Indigenous Australian Homeland, Aboriginal Studies Press, Canberra. With Sharon D’Amico, Nancy Daiyi, Kathy Deveraux, Margy Daiyi, Linda Ford and April Bright.\nL55: Second Edition (first edition: 2002) 2009 Dingo Makes Us Human; Life and land in an Australian Aboriginal Culture. New in Paperback, Cambridge University Press (third printing, digital edition) 2005 Dislocating the Frontier: Essaying the Mystique of the Outback, co-edited with Richard Davis, ANU E-Press, Canberra. 2004 Reports from a Wild Country: Ethics for Decolonisation, University of New South Wales Press, Sydney. Shortlisted for the NSW Premier’s Award and the Gleebooks Prize for Critical Writing.\n"
